    "content": "Heavy rains that began last night and continued into this morning have affected Mogadishu and several other areas across Somalia, according to reports. The downpour, which lasted for hours, caused flooding in many parts of the capital, leaving roads impassable and disrupting daily movement.\n\nThe rains come as Somalia enters the Deyr season, a period typically associated with increased rainfall. In Mogadishu, the flooding was particularly noticeable in low-lying areas, where water accumulated and made transportation difficult for both vehicles and pedestrians.\n\nNo casualties or major damage have been reported so far, but the rains have highlighted ongoing challenges with drainage infrastructure in the city. Similar weather patterns are expected to continue in the coming days, according to seasonal forecasts.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, these seasonal rains are a reminder of the vulnerability of communities back home, especially in informal settlements where flooding can exacerbate existing hardships. Many in the diaspora have family and friends in affected areas and often follow such events closely, sometimes mobilizing support.\n\nThe Deyr rains are crucial for agriculture but can also bring destruction when they are unusually heavy. The situation in Mogadishu and other regions will be monitored as the season progresses.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/16/roobk-deyrta-oo-ka-curtay-muqdisho-iyo-deegaanno-kale-oo-ka-tirsan-dalka/",
