    "original_title": "Aqalka Sare oo Dib u Eegaya Hindise-Sharciga Hay’adda Saadaasha Hawada iyo Cimilada",
    "slug": "somali-senate-committee-reviews-bill-to-establish-national-weather-and-climate-agency",
    "summary": "A Senate committee met with the Deputy Minister of Environment to discuss a bill that would create Somalia's first dedicated weather and climate services agency.",
    "content": "On September 15, 2026, the Somali Senate's National Resources, Infrastructure, Economy, and Transport Committee held a session with Deputy Minister of Environment Hussein Ali Haji to examine a proposed law that would establish a new agency for weather and climate services in Somalia. The meeting focused on the specific provisions of the draft legislation, with committee members posing questions and seeking clarifications on several articles.\n\nThe bill aims to create a national body responsible for meteorological and climatological services, a function that is currently scattered across various government departments and international partners. The committee's review is part of the legislative process, where lawmakers scrutinize the draft before it advances to the full Senate for debate.\n\nSomalia has long lacked a unified institution to monitor weather patterns and climate risks, despite being highly vulnerable to droughts, floods, and other climate-related shocks. The proposed agency would consolidate these services under one authority, potentially improving early warning systems and climate data collection.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the establishment of such an agency is significant. Many diaspora communities are directly affected by climate disasters in Somalia, often mobilizing emergency relief for relatives back home. A centralized weather and climate agency could provide more reliable forecasts, enabling earlier and more coordinated diaspora responses to droughts and floods. Additionally, diaspora professionals in meteorology, environmental science, and related fields may find new opportunities to contribute to Somalia's climate resilience through this agency.\n\nThe committee's scrutiny of the bill signals a step forward in legislative efforts to address climate governance. The outcome of this review will determine whether the bill proceeds to a full parliamentary vote. If passed, the agency could begin operations in the coming year, marking a major institutional development for Somalia's environmental management.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/15/aqalka-sare-oo-dib-u-eegaya-hindise-sharciga-hayadda-saadaasha-hawada-iyo-cimilada/",
