    "id": "news-1789497362559-8",
    "title": "IFC Invests $2.8 Million in KIMS Microfinance to Expand Islamic Finance in Somalia",
    "original_title": "IFC oo $2.8 Milyan ku Maalgelisay KIMS Microfinance",
    "slug": "ifc-invests-2-8-million-in-kims-microfinance-to-expand-islamic-finance-in-somalia",
    "summary": "The International Finance Corporation (IFC) has made its first investment in Somalia, providing $2.8 million in Islamic-compliant financing to KIMS Microfinance to expand financial access.",
    "content": "The International Finance Corporation (IFC) has announced its first investment in Somalia, allocating $2.8 million in Sharia-compliant financing to KIMS Microfinance. The package includes $1 million from IFC and $1.8 million from AtoZ Foundation and Symbiotics, with a partial guarantee from the IDA20 Private Sector Window. This marks a significant step in international financial engagement with Somalia.\n\nKIMS Microfinance, a local institution, plans to use the funds to increase its client base from approximately 5,200 borrowers to 10,000. The financing adheres to Islamic finance principles, which prohibit interest and emphasize risk-sharing and ethical investment. This approach is particularly relevant in Somalia, where the majority of the population is Muslim and many prefer Sharia-compliant financial services.\n\nThe investment comes amid ongoing efforts to rebuild Somalia's financial sector after decades of conflict and instability. Access to credit remains limited, with only a small fraction of the population using formal banking services. Microfinance institutions like KIMS play a critical role in providing loans to small businesses and individuals who lack access to traditional banks.\n\nThe IFC's involvement signals growing confidence in Somalia's private sector and could attract further international investment. For the Somali diaspora, which sends billions of dollars in remittances annually, this development may open new avenues for supporting local businesses and economic development. A more robust microfinance sector can also reduce reliance on informal money transfer systems and foster financial inclusion.\n\nThe expansion of KIMS Microfinance is expected to create economic opportunities, particularly for women and small-scale entrepreneurs who often face barriers to obtaining credit. By doubling its customer base, KIMS aims to extend financial services to underserved communities, potentially spurring job creation and local economic growth.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/15/ifc-oo-2-8-milyan-ku-maalgelisay-kims-microfinance/",
