    "id": "news-1789480796782-2",
    "title": "Wild Animals Destroy Crops in Togdher, Leaving 150 Farmers Without Income",
    "original_title": "Wild animal invade fenceless farms in Togdher ruining families’ crops and incomes",
    "slug": "wild-animals-destroy-crops-in-togdher-leaving-150-farmers-without-income",
    "summary": "Around 150 farmers in Somaliland's Togdher region have halted cultivation after wild animals destroyed their crops, worsening food shortages since June.",
    "content": "Around 150 farmers in the Sheikh district of Somaliland's Togdher region have been unable to cultivate their land since June, following repeated crop destruction by wild animals, including monkeys and wild pigs. The farmers, who rely on these fields for their livelihoods, have lost two consecutive harvests and now face growing food shortages.\n\nThe problem stems from a lack of fencing. Without adequate barriers, animals have been able to enter the farmland freely. In the villages of Agarey, Himin, and Bodhlay, farmers traditionally built fences from local trees, but deforestation has left them without materials. Wire fencing is too expensive for most families, according to Ahmed Omar Ige, chairman of the farmers' cooperative.\n\nAhmed Omar Ige explained that only proper fencing can keep the animals out, but the community lacks the resources to construct it. The situation has forced many farmers to abandon their fields, as they cannot afford to lose another season's crops.\n\nThis crisis is part of a broader pattern of environmental and economic pressures facing rural communities in Somaliland. Deforestation, driven by demand for charcoal and fuel, has reduced the availability of traditional fencing materials. At the same time, erratic rainfall and ongoing food insecurity have made it harder for families to recover from agricultural losses.\n\nThe impact is particularly severe for the Somali diaspora, many of whom have relatives in Togdher and other agricultural regions. Remittances from abroad often serve as a lifeline during such crises, but sustained crop failures could increase the burden on diaspora families. Additionally, the loss of local food production contributes to higher food prices and reduced availability in markets that diaspora communities rely on for imports.\n\nLocal officials and aid organizations have yet to announce a coordinated response. Farmers like those in Sheikh continue to call for assistance to rebuild fences and restore their ability to farm. Without intervention, the food shortages are likely to worsen, affecting not only the farmers but also the wider community that depends on their produce.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/09/wild-animal-invade-fenceless-farms-in-togdher-ruining-families-crops-and-incomes/",
