    "title": "Houthis Launch New Attacks Inside Saudi Arabia, Stalling Hormuz Diplomacy",
    "original_title": "Xuutiyiinta oo weerarro cusub ku qaaday Sacuudiga",
    "slug": "houthis-launch-new-attacks-inside-saudi-arabia-stalling-hormuz-diplomacy",
    "summary": "Yemen's Houthi movement fired missiles and drones at Saudi Arabia's Khamis Mushait air base on Monday, as diplomatic efforts to reopen the Strait of Hormuz stalled.",
    "content": "The Houthi movement in Yemen carried out a new round of attacks inside Saudi Arabia on Monday, targeting the Khamis Mushait air base in the south of the country. According to Reuters, the group said it launched more than a dozen missiles and drones at the facility, aiming at aircraft hangars, radar systems, runways, and other military infrastructure. There was no immediate confirmation from Saudi authorities about the extent of the damage or any casualties.\n\nThe attack comes amid a resurgence of fighting in Yemen and a pause in diplomatic negotiations aimed at reopening the Strait of Hormuz, a vital waterway for global oil shipments. The Houthis have previously targeted Saudi territory, but this strike appears to be one of the more significant in recent months. The group has not commented on the status of the diplomatic talks, which had raised hopes for a de-escalation in the region.\n\nKhamis Mushait air base is a key military installation located in southwestern Saudi Arabia, near the border with Yemen. It has been a frequent target of Houthi attacks since the Saudi-led coalition intervened in Yemen's civil war in 2015. The Houthis, who control large parts of northern Yemen, have used ballistic missiles and drones to strike inside Saudi Arabia, often claiming to target military sites. Saudi Arabia has accused Iran of supplying the Houthis with weapons, a charge Tehran denies.\n\nThe Strait of Hormuz is a critical chokepoint for global oil trade, with about a fifth of the world's oil passing through it daily. Any disruption to shipping there could have far-reaching consequences for energy markets and the global economy. Diplomatic efforts to ensure safe passage through the strait had been underway, but the latest attacks suggest those talks have stalled. The Houthis have not explicitly linked the attack to the negotiations, but the timing raises questions about the prospects for a diplomatic breakthrough.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the renewed hostilities in Yemen and the threat to the Strait of Hormuz are a matter of concern. Many Somalis rely on remittances and trade routes that pass through the region, and any escalation could affect shipping costs and the availability of goods in Somalia. Additionally, Somali refugees and migrants in Yemen may face increased risks as the conflict intensifies. The Somali government has not yet commented on the situation, but regional observers are watching closely.\n\nThe attack underscores the fragile security situation in the Horn of Africa and the wider region. With diplomatic efforts stalled and fighting ongoing, the risk of further escalation remains high. The international community has called for restraint, but so far, there is no indication that either side is willing to back down. The situation remains fluid, and further developments are expected in the coming days.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/15/xuutiyiinta-oo-weerarro-cusub-ku-qaaday-sacuudiga/",
    "source": "goobjoog.com",
